      <h2>Understanding the 8th Pay Commission Framework</h2>
      <p>
        The conversation surrounding the <strong>8th Pay Commission (8th CPC)</strong> has become central to planning among employees across Central Ministries, Defence services, Indian Railways, and State administrations that align with central pay parity. As inflation indices rise and existing Dearness Allowance (DA) thresholds compound, calculating your revised take-home pay requires a solid understanding of how pay matrices evolve.
      </p>
      <p>
        Historically, Central Pay Commissions are instituted once every decade to revise base compensation, adjust retirement benefits, and update structural allowances in line with real economic growth:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>5th Pay Commission (1996):</strong> Consolidated earlier pay structures and instituted modern DA indexation formulas.</li>
        <li><strong>6th Pay Commission (2006):</strong> Popularized the Pay Band plus Grade Pay categorization system.</li>
        <li><strong>7th Pay Commission (2016):</strong> Discontinued Grade Pay terminology, replacing it with an 18-level consolidated Pay Matrix while raising the minimum entry-level basic pay from ₹7,000 to ₹18,000 using a 2.57x fitment multiplier.</li>
        <li><strong>8th Pay Commission:</strong> Scheduled to revise cell figures across all 18 levels, absorb accumulated DA into an expanded basic pay, and adjust baseline ceilings for statutory benefits.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Fitment Factor: How the Multiplier Operates</h2>
      <p>
        The heart of any <strong>8th pay commission salary calculator</strong> is the <strong>fitment factor</strong>. This uniform numerical multiplier scales an employee's existing basic pay into the updated matrix framework. It captures two variables at once: the complete merger of prevailing Dearness Allowance into the base salary, and the net real wage expansion awarded above inflation.
      </p>
      
