      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>House Rent Allowance (HRA):</strong> HRA tier percentages (currently 30%, 20%, and 10% for Class X, Y, and Z cities) will apply directly to the new, expanded basic pay, significantly increasing the actual rupee benefit.</li>
        <li><strong>Pension Parity:</strong> Civil service pensioners will have their basic pensions notionally re-indexed using the finalized fitment factor, updating pensions while resetting Dearness Relief (DR) to 0%.</li>
        <li><strong>Gratuity Enhancements:</strong> While the 7th CPC ceiling was scaled to ₹25 Lakhs upon DA exceeding 50%, recommendations for the 8th Pay Commission are expected to propose raising the maximum statutory gratuity ceiling to between ₹30 Lakhs and ₹40 Lakhs.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>
      <h3>Will Dearness Allowance (DA) become zero after the 8th Pay Commission?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes. In every pay commission cycle, accumulated DA is merged into the base salary through the fitment multiplier. The DA index is then reset to 0% and starts accumulating fresh adjustments every six months based on subsequent AICPI-IW index figures.
      </p>

      <h3>How will this affect state government employees?</h3>
      <p>
        While Central Pay Commissions apply directly to central staff and defence forces, state governments typically implement matching revisions through dedicated state pay committees or official Government Orders (GOs) within 6 to 18 months following central notifications.
      </p>

